Contradiction-First Loop

SKILL.md
Description
A TRIZ-based engine for design, development, and vibe-coding. It's a thin layer of thinking placed BEFORE generation: it turns a task into a contradiction and an ideal, then diverges along proven moves instead of settling for a generic "middle-ground" compromise. Make sure to use this skill whenever the user: frames a trade-off ("how do I combine X and Y", "simple but powerful", "fast but flexible"), says "too much on screen / cluttered / overloaded", designs an interface, flow, component, or architecture, is vibe-coding and at risk of a templated result, argues about defaults vs. control, or asks to "strip the excess / simplify without losing power". Runs standalone by default; bridges to JTBD / design thinking / Lean / ADR only when a real gap or risk calls for it. To run it on a real task, ask the user for the Input Brief (see below). After the loop, a mandatory validation gate must pass before the result is handed off downstream to copy (ux-copy) and humanizer skills — no unvalidated result passes.

Contradiction-First Loop
What this is
TRIZ translated into the language of product, interfaces, and vibe-coding. It's a thin layer of thinking that slots in before the prompt / before generation. It's not a replacement for design craft, and not full ARIZ.

It does one thing: it stops you from collapsing a task into a compromise, forces you to name the contradiction and the ideal, and hands you a finite set of divergence moves.

What you get (the deliverable)
Running the loop produces, before any code or mockup:

A named contradiction (the real tension, not a compromise).
An ideal (the outcome where the function exists but the mechanism is gone).
2–3 genuinely different directions to solve it (not one polished guess).
Acceptance criteria — the contradiction itself becomes the test the final solution must pass.
Only then do you generate. The output is a solution that dissolves the trade-off instead of splitting the difference.

How it helps
Generation (AI, vibe-coding) is strong at production and weak at framing: the model converges fast on something plausible, but usually generic and shaped as a "middle ground." This loop hits exactly there — it names the tension before generation and turns it into an acceptance test after. That's the whole mechanism.

The full pipeline (where the loop sits)
The loop is one stage in a chain. Upstream frames the problem; the loop resolves it; a validation gate guards the exit so nothing unvalidated moves on; downstream turns the resolved structure into human words and ships it. Each stage must pass before the next begins — no empty runs.

  (only if the "why" is unknown)      JTBD / research ─┐
  (only if the problem is unframed)   Design Thinking ─┤
                                                       ▼
              ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
              │   CONTRADICTION-FIRST LOOP  (moves 1–5)        │
              │   contradiction → ideal → resources →          │
              │   diverge → generate & check                   │
              └───────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                       ▼
              ▣ VALIDATION GATE ──── FAIL ──► back to Diverge (or add a bridge)
                                       │ PASS
                                       ▼
              Generate result is now structure + logic, no copy
                                       ▼
              Copy layer   →  ux-copy skill   (labels, errors, empty states, CTAs)
                                       ▼
              Humanize     →  humanizer skill (remove AI tells, tighten wording)
                                       ▼
              Ship         →  Lean / MVP      (validate the hypothesis)
The loop owns the skeleton (what the interface does and how it's structured). It deliberately does not write the words — that's the copy layer. See the validation gate and handoff below.

When to use / when to leave it alone
Use it for: structural and interaction forks, trade-offs, "overloaded screen," scope decisions, architecture arguments, "simple vs. powerful."

Don't force it on: visual taste and aesthetics, needs research, copywriting, tone/emotion. Those need other tools — this loop hands off to them, it doesn't replace them.

Standalone vs. bridged — default is standalone
The loop is self-contained. Run it alone unless a specific gap appears. If your problem is already framed, just run the five moves — no JTBD or design-thinking ceremony required. Adding frameworks "to be thorough" is process theater; it slows you down and nobody follows it.

Add a bridge only when it removes a real unknown or a real risk:

You don't actually know the user's real problem or why → pull in JTBD / research first (symptom: you're guessing at the ideal).
The fix might cause downstream side-effects → sanity-check with systems thinking after step 5 (symptom: "resolving this might break something else").
The decision needs to be recorded → dump the contradiction + chosen move into an ADR (symptom: "we'll forget why we did this").
You're shipping to test a hypothesis → hand the output to Lean / MVP (symptom: "we're not sure this is even wanted").
Rule of thumb: if a bridge doesn't kill a specific unknown or a specific risk, skip it.

What to give it — the Input Brief
To run the loop on a real task, provide as much of this as you can. The loop runs on items 1 + 3 alone, but items 2, 4, and 6 sharply improve the output. More concrete = better.

Task in one line. What are you designing or building?
Current state. What exists now? Give a link (Figma / repo / live URL), a screenshot, or a description. If nothing exists yet, say "greenfield."
The tension you feel. Two things pulling apart (X vs. Y). A rough version is fine — sharpening it is step 1.
Users. Who uses this, and which segments pull in different directions (e.g., first-timers vs. power users).
Constraints. Platform, tech stack, brand / design system, time budget, non-negotiables.
Definition of "good." What does success look like? What must be true for you to accept the result?
Resources on hand. Existing components, design tokens, data, APIs, prior art you can reuse.
Optional — the "why." Link to JTBD / research / user feedback if you have it (this feeds the ideal). If the why is unknown, flag it — that's the signal to add a JTBD bridge.
Form: plain text, links, and screenshots all work. You don't need all eight — send what you have and note what's missing.

Procedure — 5 moves
Each move is both a thinking step and a prompt pattern for AI.

1. Contradiction — name the tension, don't smooth it. Form: "improve X → Y breaks" or "the element must be both A and not-A." Example: the panel must be simple for a novice and dense for a pro. → If it comes out as a compromise, reframe it as a contradiction. That reframe is the whole point.

2. Ideal (IFR) — describe the outcome where the function exists but the mechanism doesn't. The system does it itself: no screen, no cost, no harm. This is the ceiling. Questions: what if it just happens? what if there's no screen at all? what if we output it instead of asking? → If bridging from JTBD, the ideal's direction = the needed progress achieved with zero effort.

3. Resources — what already exists. Existing components, state, user context, data, design system. Solve with these before adding new UI or a new service.

4. Diverge — apply the moves (2–3 directions, not one). For "both/and" UI dilemmas, start with the 4 separations (below). Then the move deck (references/deck.md). Goal of this step: produce several different directions, not polish the first one.

5. Generate & Check — the contradiction becomes the acceptance test. Only now generate code/mockup. Then check the result against step 1: did the solution dissolve both sides of the contradiction — or just find a compromise? → Main defense against "the AI produced something generic." Compromise = fail; rerun with a different move.

The 4 separations — the flagship tool for interfaces
The class of dilemmas "must be both simple and powerful" is dissolved by exactly this:

In time → progressive disclosure, onboarding vs. expert mode, wizards, "little first — reveal later."
In space → separate panels / surfaces / modes, split-view, a dedicated advanced area.
By condition → adaptive / role-based / contextual UI, feature flags; state depends on user and context.
Whole / parts → smart defaults (whole) + customization (parts): works out of the box, everything is tunable.
Validation gate — run before any handoff
The loop must not pass an unvalidated result downstream. After move 5, run this fixed gate. It produces a single PASS / FAIL. Only a PASS may proceed to the copy layer. This is what turns "we ran the loop" into "the loop actually resolved it."

Every item must read YES:

Contradiction dissolved? Both sides of the move-1 tension hold at once — it is not a compromise. (The core test; if NO, the rest is moot.)
Ideal-aligned? The result moves toward the move-2 ideal — it didn't quietly drift back to "just add another screen / step / setting."
Resource-efficient? Solved mostly with existing resources (move 3), not a pile of new UI or services.
Real divergence behind it? The chosen direction was picked from 2–3 genuinely different options, not the first idea.
No new contradiction created? Resolving this didn't break something elsewhere (second-order check).
Constraint-safe? Respects the brief's hard constraints (platform, tech, brand, time).
On FAIL — do not hand off. Route back:

Fail #1 or #4 → back to move 4 (Diverge) with a different move.
Fail #2, and you keep guessing the ideal → add a JTBD bridge.
Fail #5 → add a systems-thinking bridge, then re-diverge.
Fail #3 or #6 → re-solve within the available resources / constraints.
Only when all six read YES does the result exit the loop.

After the gate — from structure to words
A PASS means the skeleton is sound, but it's still structure and logic, not finished copy. Hand off in order:

ux-copy — write the interface words: labels, button text, error messages, empty states, CTAs. Short and clear.
humanizer — strip AI tells and tighten, so it reads like a person wrote it.
Ship → Lean / MVP — validate the hypothesis in the real world.
The loop gives the bones; ux-copy gives the voice; humanizer removes the robot from the voice. Keep these as separate stages — don't fold copywriting into the loop.

Use cases
Mixed-audience dashboard — "scannable for execs, deep for analysts." → separations (time / space / condition).
Onboarding — "collect enough setup, but don't overwhelm." → separation in time (progressive) + preliminary action (smart defaults).
Settings / config panel — "simple defaults, full control." → whole/parts separation.
Generic vibe-coded output — a component came back templated. Feed the contradiction as the acceptance test and rerun with a specific move.
Feature-scope creep — use the ideal to ask "what if this happens without the feature?" → cut it.
Architecture fork — "performance vs. flexibility," "consistency vs. availability." → inversion, segmentation, dynamics.
Search / filter UI — "powerful querying, not intimidating." → intermediary (query builder) + self-service + another dimension.
Two full step-by-step runs (filled brief → 5 moves → output) are in references/example.md: a mixed-audience dashboard and a SaaS onboarding flow. Read them to see what a good test case looks like.

Success conditions — the loop delivers when…
There's a real trade-off (not a trivial task with an obvious answer).
You can state the problem concretely (Input Brief items 1 + 3 at minimum).
You actually diverge — pull moves from different groups, produce 2–3 directions.
You run the step-5 check and reject compromises.
If it doesn't work — what to refine
Output still generic → the contradiction is too vague. Sharpen step 1; that's usually the fix.
Only one direction came out → you didn't diverge. Pull moves from different deck groups.
The solution breaks something else → add the systems-thinking bridge; check second-order effects.
You keep guessing at the ideal → you don't know the user's why. Add a JTBD / research bridge (brief item 8).
It feels like overkill → the task had no real trade-off. Drop the loop and just build it.
Bridges (use only when justified — see rule above)
Framework	What it adds	Where it meets the loop
JTBD	Which job, what progress, the "why"	Feeds the ideal: ideal = the job with zero struggle. Entry into the loop.
Design Thinking	Empathy, framed problem, testing	The loop occupies the Ideate step (its weak spot). Define → loop → Prototype.
First-principles	Break to fundamentals, drop "how it's done"	Kin to the ideal; both shed inherited constraints. Reinforces step 2.
Systems thinking	Links, loops, second-order effects	Catches side-effects; checks whether dissolving one contradiction creates a new one.
Lean / MVP	Hypothesis, cheap validation	Accepts the step-5 output; the "cheap/disposable" move = throwaway prototype.
ADR	Records the decision and context	Step-1 contradiction = "Context/Decision"; moves = "Alternatives."
Nielsen heuristics / design system	Usability and consistency	Applied AFTER divergence, on polish. The loop doesn't replace them.
Best practices
Run light by default. Most tasks need one sentence of contradiction + the ideal + the 4 separations. Reach for the full deck only when that isn't enough.
Spend your effort on step 1. A sharp contradiction is ~80% of the value. Vague contradiction → generic output, every time.
Force divergence. Pull 2–3 moves from different deck groups. One move = a variation, not a real alternative.
Make the acceptance check non-negotiable. Skip step 5 and you drift back to compromise.
Let the ideal be "unreasonable." IFR is a direction, not a spec. "No screen at all" is a legitimate answer worth chasing.
Prefer resources over new UI. New surfaces are the last resort.
Anti-patterns
Running the full loop on a trivial task with no real trade-off → just build it.
Adding JTBD/DT/Lean "for completeness" when the problem is already framed → process theater.
Using the loop for aesthetics, tone, or research → wrong tool.
Accepting the first generated result without the step-5 check → you'll ship a compromise.
Stacking many moves into one design → mud, not clarity. Diverge, then choose.
Positioning vs. alternatives
This method is not a rival to TRIZ — it is TRIZ, trimmed and translated for product/UI/vibe-coding. Its real neighbors are other structured solution-generation methods: SCAMPER (a move checklist, but no contradiction/ideal logic), full TRIZ (same logic, far heavier), and brainstorming (fast but unstructured). It sits in the "generate the solution / by proven moves" quadrant; problem-framing methods (JTBD, Design Thinking) sit elsewhere and feed into it.

Its wedge: resolves a trade-off (not just spawns ideas), stays lightweight, and is AI-native with a built-in check against generic output. It loses when the problem isn't framed yet (use JTBD / Design Thinking) or when raw wide divergence is all you need (brainstorming is faster).

